<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head><title>404 Not Found</title></head>
  <body>
    <h1>Not Found</h1>
    <p>
      This file has a .png name and is an HTML error page. That is deliberate, and the
      NAME says so: §5.1(h)'s convention is that a fixture which breaks a rule on purpose
      declares itself in its filename, the way missing-on-purpose.png and does-not-exist
      already do. fixture-integrity.test.ts allows this one name and still fails on every
      other image whose bytes disagree with its extension - which is the accident it
      exists to catch, the v2 extension converting fixtures in place.

      It probes as ProbeSkipCode 'not-an-image', and it is the pattern's blocker (R138).

      The blocker used to be theme-dark.png: a 1x1 PNG that webp 80 grew from 70 bytes to
      94. That reason depended on ENCODER BEHAVIOUR, the most changeable thing in the
      system. R79 had already measured that class as nearly empty - 56 combinations, WebP
      won every one - and kept this fixture as its last member, and R131's lossless mode
      emptied it: the same 1x1 encodes losslessly to 36 bytes, so it converts.

      There is no replacement image either. Across 471 real PNGs, zero defeat both
      encodes: a minimal PNG is ~67 bytes against lossless WebP's ~36-byte floor, and on
      larger images lossless WebP beats PNG by design.

      R129 met three real instances of THIS shape in the corpus - rg-lisboa-header.png,
      szrubyist.png and rg-nairobi-header.png - so it is the commonest real cause rather
      than a contrivance, and no encoder improvement can ever undo it.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
